The plan for implementing CTRL-K:
0. I want to directly manipulate the DOM here. Think about this - what do I need here? I need to add an onkeydown listener to the document itself. I want the listner to be in use for as long as the document exists. Let me think more. The DOM is the outside world and I need to synchronise something with it. Effects it is.
1. Use an effect to directly manipulate the DOM and attach an onkeydown listener for CTRL-K. Direct DOM manipulation is one of the primary use cases for an effect. Why an effect though and why not a straight function? Remember that we need effects for things we want to synchronise with the DOM. Effects let us run code that synchronise our component to something else; in this case we need to synchronise our onkeydown listener with the DOM. This listener needs to exist for as long as the component is mounted, and get cleaned up if it ever unmounts. Well realistically speaking here it will never unmount as I will attach it in App.svelte, but for discipline it's good to return a cleanup function. In our case we will have a removeEventListener to remove the onkeydown event listener from the document.
2. Declare a component instance that I will be passing down to the effect to call a function on it. This instance would be of the Search component. This instance would help us get a reference to the search component and allow us to interact programmatically with it.
3. To achieve the component instance thing, we will first export a function inside Search.svelte which will call .focus() on the input element inside Search.svlete. How will we call .focus() on the input? We need a reference to the input element too - for this we will use a bind:this on the input elment.
That's it.

