2026-08-18
I am back again. I looked at my previous react (eww) code, specifically the part about fetching API data for the pokemon sprites. I see that I have used an effect there as well (correctly) and have set to the state (or in svelte words, updated the state) INSIDE the effect. In my note on Reactivity I have written "If you absolutely have to update $state within an effect (YOU SHOULD NEVER DO THAT)...". That's a conflict to me. Why do I need the data to be a state here? Should it be state? Let me think more about this; but before that something else. Yes, I have used the async/await form of a function inside useEffect in my old code too, which sets to the state the value of the fetched data. This is what I have done inside my $effect too - minus the part about updating the state.
Another thing to think about is how the function/callback used for $effect CANNOT have async attached to it. Think about this - an $effect returns a cleanup function which is a callback, while an async function ALWAYS returns a promise. Those two are incompatible, so we're left with either of these things:
1. Create a function inside $effect which itself is async in nature and calls your API and updates the data on some variable that is declared outside the effect. This async function makes sure that the fetching part is deferred until it's actually done. Updating a variable's value only after the fetching is done (i.e. after an await response.json()) makes sure we don't have that variable as undefined, as we have (a)waited long enough for the fetching to be done.
2. Create a promise chain. This is because fetching from an API endpoint is essentially returning a promise object to us. How do you resolve a promise? Using .then() which only works AFTER the promise is resolved. Thus you could have something like "fetch data, .then run result.json() on the result, .then assign that JSON value to some external variable". Keep in mind that async/await is just syntactic sugar for this .then().
Those are the two ways a successful fetch could work. In my previous code I had opted for (1). And in my current code I have written about both methods, but have chosen (1) as the thing I want to do (because async/await looks nicer). Nevertheless, I have commented out the alternative valid approach, that is (2).
Okay, now let me think more again. Should data be state and should I update it inside the effect? That is the question.
Let's think about dependencies for a second. Effects are functions that run when the state updates. Effects run after the component has been mounted to the DOM. $effect automatically picks up any reactive values (like $state, $derived, $props) that are SYNCHRONOUSLY read inside its function body, including those that are more indirect, via function calls. It registers them as dependencies, and when these dependencies change, the $effect schedules a re-run. Values that are read ASYNCHRONOUSLY, like after an await or inside a setTimeout will NOT be tracked. Let me go back to my APIdata example from before as that was causing an infinite loop. Let's see why - APIdata (state btw) was assigned the value of a new JSON object. This means that state has changed, so the effect schedules a re-run. I then used a console.log using $state.snapshot to read the state APIdata. I can see why this causes an infinite loop - as the read caused another re-run, and that caused another re-run and so on.. Thus there were infinite console logs (well not really, the svelte copiler warns you before things blow up). The compiler told me that perhaps the value in question should not be state; I agreed. But now I am reading that values that are read asynchronously, like after an await will not be tracked and will NOT schedule a re-run. This means that if I update my state inside an async function it will not schedule a re-run and thus there's no infinite loop. Hence, this pattern is completely valid, and might be a valid use case for an $effect.
Let me now think about why data should be state at all. Let me go back to the react docs and think about what they told me - if a variable is NOT unchanged over time, passed in from parent via props, or computable, it should be state. Is data changed over time? It's certainly not computable or passed in from parents. I think that yes, the data changes over time - as at the start it's undefined but after the effect runs it's well defined and is a JSON object. So it's NOT unchanged. Thus, it satisfies teh criteria for being a state.
Another thing to think about is whether I want to use $effect.pre here. It's only used when I want to run some code BEFORE the DOM updates. I think fetching from an API before the DOM updates is good? I will have to attach some handling for this though, like a loading thumbnail on everything. Note that this effect runs when the components are NOT mounted to the DOM - so there's nothing to put the loading handle on. Well, is that what I really want? I don't know. Likely not. 
So the conclusion is - updating the state (or reading to it) ASYNCHRONOUSLY inside an effect is a valid pattern as it doesn't register it as a dependency as it's untracked. 
Okay, back to what I was doing - let me fetch the data properly first.
Boy do I have a LOT to say. I am taking a break before all of this. I have written a whole lot of notes. I found out that effect was NOT the best tool for this job and how I resolved the "should the data be state" problem entirely.

I am back! I was packing things up because I was leaving for my dorm room tomorrow. But I am back now and I will explain some of the insights and decisions I have learned about.
First of all, let's address the elephant in the room - $effect. I have decided to NOT use $effect for syncing data with the API endpoint. That is, I am NOT using an $effect for fetching data from the API. Why? That's a good question and I've got multiple reasons for it. Firstly, it's a whole lot of work. If I use an $effect, and then later render the data, I will experience a slight delay beteween the components rendering (because the data is being fetched after mount, as the effect runs after mount). Now this can be easily handled using an {#if} block. I could definitely do that, but I refuse to, because it's more importantly non-idiomatic. There's a pythonic way to do things in python (think list comprehensions), and there's a Svelte-y way to do things in Svelte. If this were React, I would be totally happy with my $effect doing the API fetching. But I want the framework to do things for me - that's the whole reason it's a framework, it's there to make my life easier. So how does Svelte make my life easier here? Using an {#await} block! Yes, it too uses an $effect under the hood, but it's a more idiomatic way. No {#if} blocks to check whether my variable is undefined befoer being assigned in the $effect, and no checking for errors manually in the async code. The await block does all of that for me - it's like an async function inside an effect but more "optimised" maybe, because it's baked into the framework itself. I don't know about how "faster" it is though and I wouldn't get into that.
Anyways, just to show that it's possible I am going to be implementing my entire function USING AN EFFECT. Isn't this the thing I said I would not do? Yes. But I want to show you that it's totally legal and you CAN do that. The reason I will NOT do it in my acutal UI is because of totally personal preferences - I prefer to take full advantage of the framework - that's it. Let me go implement the $effect version of fetching now. 
Okay as I am implementing this I am trying to show that the components should only be rendered when finalData is not null. Now finalData is a normal variable (not state) and thus even if I have wrapped my code around in an {#if} block to check whether finalData is undefined or not, I am seeing nothing! This is because the code runs SYNCHRONOUSLY and at the time of definition finalData is null. Thus, it should be state. Being state means that when I update it the if block will be able to catch the updated value and see whether the components should be rendered. 
Okay, I made it state. AS EXPECTED!!! There's a tiny delay in the components rendering because the effect runs after mount. And yes it works perfectly fine otherwise. Yes I could add a loading screen here using an else block as I have discussed before but I won't do that - IT'S NOT IDIOMATIC! I just prefer to use await blocks - they handle everything for me - no manually writing if/else conditions.
Anyways, I will commit this because I am learning this as I build it.
I am back! I have committed it. I have also been reminded of the docs saying that you should not synchronise state inside $effects. That's just a bad pattern and is non-idiomatic in Svelte. I am taking that at face value for both synchronous and asynchronous synchronizations.
Let me remove the smelly code now - it was just there for documentation and is now safely committed.

Hmm. Now there's a lot to explain. It would just be better if you looked at my notes for this. Those explain why I have chosen to assgin the function to a variable and then assign that variable (which is a promise) to the await block. In short, it's because if the await block just had fetchDataFromEndpoint() there would be a scope issue - I would not be able to access the JSON data inside my <script> as it's scoped to the markup. This is the reason I give a promise object to await block instead, and use a .then() on that to assign the resolved JSON to a variable. This makes the asynchronous fetch work for both the await block (markup) and my <script>. I think I should also tell you that async/await is also valid for "unwrapping" the promise (it's literally a syntactic sugar over .then), I just happen to prefer .then() since it's less code.

